# =============================================================================
# BioPolicy — environment configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `api/config.py` validates this at import time and
# refuses to start if anything required is missing, rather than failing later
# with a confusing 500 from deep inside a provider call.
#
# NOTHING in this file may be prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ except where explicitly
# marked. Anything NEXT_PUBLIC_ is shipped to the browser in cleartext.
# =============================================================================

# --- runtime -----------------------------------------------------------------
APP_ENV=development                 # development | preview | production
LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- Supabase ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Project URL, e.g. https://abcdefghijkl.supabase.co
SUPABASE_URL=
# Server-side only. Bypasses RLS entirely — never expose to the browser.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
# Used by the API only to verify user JWTs.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# Direct Postgres connection (pooler URI from the Supabase dashboard).
DATABASE_URL=
# Storage bucket holding uploaded PDFs. Must be PRIVATE.
SUPABASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=documents

# --- Anthropic (primary LLM) -------------------------------------------------
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5-20251001

# --- Voyage (embeddings) -----------------------------------------------------
# The vector side, and the provider embeddings actually use. Anthropic has no
# embedding endpoint and points at Voyage for them, so this is a third provider
# rather than a second use of the first — see docs/adr/016.
#
# Set, it is used. Unset, embeddings fall back to Gemini at the same width, and
# the fallback is a warning in the log rather than a supported configuration:
# Google's free tier meters passages (1,000 a day, a 27-page policy is 132 of
# them), which is what drove the move.
VOYAGE_API_KEY=
VOYAGE_MODEL=voyage-4-lite

# How fast this deployment may embed. Voyage meters both, and the slower of the
# two wins.
#
# These defaults are the reduced tier an account with no payment method on file
# is held at — the API says so when it throttles ("3 RPM and 10K TPM"). At 10K
# tokens a minute a 27-page policy takes about four minutes to ingest, because
# it is 36,000 tokens. That wait is this ceiling and almost nothing else.
#
# Adding a payment method lifts it and bills nothing: the first 200M tokens are
# free regardless, and past them the rate is $0.02 per million. But the ceiling
# is enforced on both sides, so lifting it with the provider and leaving these
# alone changes nothing at all. Copy what the provider's Rate Limits page states
# for the account.
VOYAGE_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=3
VOYAGE_TOKENS_PER_MINUTE=10000

# --- Google (vision OCR + fallback LLM, and embeddings only as a fallback) ---
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
GEMINI_EMBEDDING_MODEL=gemini-embedding-001
# Verified at build time — see docs/adr/004. Do not guess this value.
GEMINI_FALLBACK_MODEL=
GEMINI_OCR_MODEL=

# --- budget & quotas ---------------------------------------------------------
# Global hard ceiling in USD. The circuit breaker disables uploads at 80%
# and drops to read-only sample mode at 100%.
GLOBAL_BUDGET_USD=30.0

# Rates for every model this deployment calls, `model:input:output` in USD per
# million tokens. Anthropic's are verified in api/pricing.py; Google's live here
# because ADR 004's rule against fabricating a model id covers its price too.
#
# A model missing from this list is NOT free. Its calls are recorded at zero and
# never reach the breaker's total, so the ceiling above would cap part of the
# bill only. `preview` and `production` refuse to start rather than run that way;
# `development` reports it as `unpriced` on /api/health.
#
# Embeddings have no output tokens, hence the zero.
MODEL_PRICES=gemini-embedding-001:0.15:0,gemini-3.6-flash:1.50:7.50
# The day someone actually looked at the pricing page. Required — a rate nobody
# can date is a rate nobody checked.
MODEL_PRICES_VERIFIED_ON=2026-08-09

# --- what this actually costs ------------------------------------------------
# Measured, not estimated, on a real 27-page AXA home policy and on the spend
# already recorded in `usage_events`:
#
#   ingesting that policy (132 passages)   $0.006   once, per document
#   one question (answer + verification)   $0.016
#   100 such documents                     $0.60
#   1,000 questions                        $16.00
#
# Where a question's money goes: 58% to producing the answer, 42% to verifying
# it. Turning verification off is therefore the single largest saving available
# and it is NOT recommended — the published evaluation shows it changing no
# decision on the clean corpus but catching a contradiction on the adversarial
# one, and it is the mechanism this project exists to demonstrate.
#
# The cheap savings, in order, all already on by default:
#   * ENABLE_ANSWER_CACHE — the sample documents get the same nine questions
#     from every visitor; a hit costs nothing at all, not even an embedding.
#   * ENABLE_RETRIEVAL_FLOOR — an off-topic question is refused before any model
#     is called.
#   * ENABLE_ENTAILMENT_CHECK=false — measured at +24% per question for no
#     decision changed on this corpus.
#
# **The limit you will actually hit is not cost, it is the embedding tier.**
# Embeddings are the cheapest call this system makes and the slowest to be
# allowed to make: that 27-page policy is 36,000 tokens, which is $0.0007 of
# Voyage's 200M free allowance and four minutes of its 10K-per-minute ceiling.
# Cost is not what constrains this deployment; `VOYAGE_TOKENS_PER_MINUTE` above
# is. The 200M would cover roughly 5,500 such policies.
#
# The line below applies only to the Gemini fallback, whose quota counts
# passages rather than tokens — 1,000 a day, so about seven uploads.
EMBED_TEXTS_PER_MINUTE=100
# Per account, per day, checked BEFORE the provider call. Small on purpose:
# every question is a real model call against a real bill.
USER_DAILY_MESSAGE_LIMIT=3
USER_DAILY_DOCUMENT_LIMIT=1

# Accounts exempt from the two limits above, comma-separated. Empty by default,
# so a deployment that forgets this grants nothing.
#
# The address is matched against auth.users at check time, never taken from a
# token, and only for a confirmed Google account (docs/adr/013). It lives here
# rather than in code because the repository is public.
# UNLIMITED_EMAILS=you@example.com

# --- ingestion limits --------------------------------------------------------
MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=26214400           # 25 MiB
MAX_PAGE_COUNT=250
# Vision OCR is billed per page image and dominates cost. Hard cap the OCR
# path well below MAX_PAGE_COUNT. See docs/adr/005.
MAX_OCR_PAGE_COUNT=30

# --- retention ---------------------------------------------------------------
RETENTION_HOURS=24
# Shared secret the scheduled purge job presents to POST /api/internal/purge.
# Generate with: python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
PURGE_JOB_SECRET=

# --- anti-hallucination toggles (the eval harness flips these) ---------------
ENABLE_CITATION_BINDING=true
ENABLE_SELF_VERIFICATION=true
# The fourth mechanism, off by default: measured as changing no decisions on the
# demo corpus while adding ~24% to the cost of every question. It does catch
# contradictions on the adversarial set — see docs/adr/014.
ENABLE_ENTAILMENT_CHECK=false
ENABLE_QUERY_REWRITE=true
ENABLE_RERANK=false

# =============================================================================
# Frontend (web/.env.local) — these ARE sent to the browser, by design.
# =============================================================================
# Copy these two into web/.env.local, with the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix. The anon key
# is designed to be public: it identifies the project and is useless without a
# valid JWT and the RLS policies behind it. The SERVICE ROLE key is the one that
# must never appear under a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name — it bypasses every policy.
#
# The browser needs these to sign in with Google (docs/adr/013). The Google
# provider is OFF by default on a new Supabase project: enable it under
# Authentication -> Sign In / Providers, or nobody can sign in.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
#
# Where the Next.js rewrite forwards /api/*. Not NEXT_PUBLIC_: it is read at
# build time in next.config.ts, never in the browser.
# API_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:8000
#
# The canonical origin, used for every absolute URL a crawler reads:
# canonical links, the sitemap, the Open Graph image. Vercel supplies
# VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL on its own, so this is only needed when the
# site is served from somewhere else — a custom domain in front of another
# host, or a local build you want to inspect the tags of.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://biopolicy.bilalgurkansanli.com
#
# Microsoft Clarity, for measuring where visitors get stuck. Entirely optional:
# left empty, no tag is ever requested and the site behaves identically.
#
# The id is a project id from clarity.microsoft.com, not a secret — it is
# visible in the page source of every site that uses Clarity.
#
# Two things this deployment does that Clarity does not do for you:
#   * The tag is loaded only after the visitor accepts, from the banner or by
#     signing in. Before that there is no request to clarity.ms at all.
#   * The workspace carries `data-clarity-mask`, so recordings keep the clicks
#     and lose the text — the documents, the questions and the answers are not
#     Clarity's to store. Set masking to "Mask all" in the dashboard as well;
#     belt and braces are cheap here.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CLARITY_PROJECT_ID=
